Below is a quick “design‑thinking” walkthrough of the key tables / columns you would normally combine to detect an on‑track positional change (“overtake”) from one lap to the next, together with ten exploratory SQLite queries (progressing from trivial to quite involved) that help you inspect and stitch the data together.

────────────────────────────────────────
1.  Relevant columns and their potential use
────────────────────────────────────────
• lap_positions.lap            : which lap we are on  
• lap_positions.position       : the car’s order as the lap is crossed  
• lap_positions.position_order : (if present) numeric equivalent of position_text  
• lap_positions.driver_id      : who the car is  
• lap_positions.race_id        : which grand prix it belongs to  
• lap_positions.lap_type       : differentiate “Race”, “Starting Grid”, “Pit In/Out” etc.  

• pit_stops.race_id / driver_id / lap  
  – identify “pit‑in” and “pit‑out” laps to filter false “overtakes” caused by pit‑stops.  

• retirements.race_id / driver_id / lap  
  – cars disappearing mid‑lap should not be counted as having been overtaken.  

• results.race_id / driver_id / position_order  
  – useful for sanity‑checking the final order of each race.  

• races_ext.is_pit_data_available = 1  
  – tells us which grands prix have complete pit‑stop logs (only evaluate those).  

Typical filtering / join conditions you will eventually need  
  – Only take races with races_ext.is_pit_data_available = 1  
  – Ignore lap_positions rows where lap_type ≠ 'Race'  
  – Remove laps during which either the passer or passee is in pit_stops  
  – Remove laps after retirements for the retired driver.  
  – Compare each driver’s position at lap = N‑1 vs lap = N to see whether he went from ahead → behind or behind → ahead relative to a given rival.  

────────────────────────────────────────
2.  Ten inspection / prototype queries
────────────────────────────────────────
(sqlite dialect – wrap identifiers in double quotes when needed)

```sql
-- 1️⃣ Description: List the grand‑prix IDs that have full pit‑stop coverage.
SELECT DISTINCT race_id
FROM races_ext
WHERE is_pit_data_available = 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2️⃣ Description: Peek at lap‑by‑lap positions for the first “pit‑data‑ready” race.
SELECT lp.*
FROM lap_positions AS lp
JOIN races_ext AS re
  ON re.race_id = lp.race_id
WHERE re.is_pit_data_available = 1
ORDER BY lp.race_id, lp.lap, lp.position
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3️⃣ Description: Show the pit‑in / pit‑out information for that same race.
SELECT DISTINCT ps.race_id,
       ps.driver_id,
       ps.lap,
       ps.stop,
       ps.time,
       ps.duration
FROM pit_stops AS ps
WHERE ps.race_id IN (
      SELECT race_id FROM races_ext WHERE is_pit_data_available = 1
)
ORDER BY ps.race_id, ps.driver_id, ps.lap
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4️⃣ Description: Verify which drivers retired in races with pit data.
SELECT DISTINCT r.race_id,
       r.driver_id,
       r.lap,
       r.retirement_type
FROM retirements AS r
WHERE r.race_id IN (
      SELECT race_id FROM races_ext WHERE is_pit_data_available = 1
)
ORDER BY r.race_id, r.driver_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5️⃣ Description: Check starting‑grid positions (lap_type = 'Starting Position - Grid Drop').
SELECT DISTINCT lp.race_id,
       lp.driver_id,
       lp.lap,
       lp.position,
       lp.lap_type
FROM lap_positions AS lp
WHERE lp.lap_type LIKE 'Starting%'
  AND lp.race_id IN (
      SELECT race_id FROM races_ext WHERE is_pit_data_available = 1
)
ORDER BY lp.race_id, lp.position
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6️⃣ Description: Isolate only pure “Race” laps (no formation, no pits) for later diffing.
SELECT DISTINCT lp.race_id,
       lp.driver_id,
       lp.lap,
       lp.position
FROM lap_positions AS lp
WHERE lp.lap_type = 'Race'
  AND lp.race_id IN (
      SELECT race_id FROM races_ext WHERE is_pit_data_available = 1
)
ORDER BY lp.race_id, lp.lap, lp.position
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7️⃣ Description: Build a minimal lap‑to‑lap delta table for one driver (proof of concept).
SELECT cur.race_id,
       cur.driver_id,
       cur.lap   AS lap_curr,
       cur.position AS pos_curr,
       prev.lap  AS lap_prev,
       prev.position AS pos_prev
FROM lap_positions AS cur
JOIN lap_positions AS prev
  ON prev.race_id = cur.race_id
 AND prev.driver_id = cur.driver_id
 AND prev.lap = cur.lap - 1
WHERE cur.lap_type  = 'Race'
  AND prev.lap_type = 'Race'
  AND cur.race_id   = 1              -- pick one race for demo
ORDER BY cur.driver_id, cur.lap
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8️⃣ Description: Identify laps in which any two drivers swap order (candidate overtakes).
SELECT a.race_id,
       a.lap,
       a.driver_id  AS driver_a,
       b.driver_id  AS driver_b,
       a.position   AS pos_a_curr,
       b.position   AS pos_b_curr,
       pa.position  AS pos_a_prev,
       pb.position  AS pos_b_prev
FROM lap_positions AS a
JOIN lap_positions AS b
  ON b.race_id   = a.race_id
 AND b.lap       = a.lap
 AND b.position  = a.position + 1     -- directly ahead of a on the same lap
JOIN lap_positions AS pa
  ON pa.race_id  = a.race_id
 AND pa.driver_id= a.driver_id
 AND pa.lap      = a.lap - 1
JOIN lap_positions AS pb
  ON pb.race_id  = b.race_id
 AND pb.driver_id= b.driver_id
 AND pb.lap      = b.lap - 1
WHERE a.lap_type = 'Race'
  AND pa.position < pb.position       -- A was ahead of B on the prev lap
  AND a.position  >  b.position       -- A is now behind B  (i.e., B overtook A)
  AND a.race_id IN (
      SELECT race_id FROM races_ext WHERE is_pit_data_available = 1
)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9️⃣ Description: Exclude situations where either driver pitted on this lap.
SELECT * FROM (
    /* use query 8 as a CTE for readability (abbreviated here) */
    WITH candidate_overtakes AS (
        SELECT a.race_id,
               a.lap,
               a.driver_id  AS driver_a,
               b.driver_id  AS driver_b
        FROM   lap_positions a
        JOIN   lap_positions b
               ON b.race_id  = a.race_id
              AND b.lap      = a.lap
              AND b.position = a.position + 1
        JOIN   lap_positions pa
               ON pa.race_id = a.race_id
              AND pa.driver_id = a.driver_id
              AND pa.lap = a.lap - 1
        JOIN   lap_positions pb
               ON pb.race_id = b.race_id
              AND pb.driver_id = b.driver_id
              AND pb.lap = b.lap - 1
        WHERE  a.lap_type = 'Race'
          AND  pa.position < pb.position      -- A ahead of B prev lap
          AND  a.position  > b.position       -- A now behind B
          AND  a.race_id IN (
               SELECT race_id FROM races_ext WHERE is_pit_data_available = 1
          )
    )
    SELECT co.*
    FROM   candidate_overtakes AS co
    LEFT JOIN pit_stops AS psA
           ON psA.race_id = co.race_id
          AND psA.driver_id = co.driver_a
          AND psA.lap = co.lap
    LEFT JOIN pit_stops AS psB
           ON psB.race_id = co.race_id
          AND psB.driver_id = co.driver_b
          AND psB.lap = co.lap
    WHERE  psA.race_id IS NULL     -- neither in the pits
      AND  psB.race_id IS NULL
    LIMIT 20
);
```

```sql
-- 🔟 Description: Count the filtered “overtake” events, grouped by type
-- (“Start‑lap”, “On‑track”, “Pit‑in”, “Pit‑out”, “Post‑retirement”).  
-- (Here we only illustrate grouping logic; classification CASE is illustrative.)
SELECT
    CASE
        WHEN co.lap = 1                                   THEN 'Race Start'
        WHEN ps_prev.driver_id IS NOT NULL                THEN 'Pit Exit'
        WHEN ps_curr.driver_id IS NOT NULL                THEN 'Pit Entry'
        WHEN ret_prev.driver_id IS NOT NULL               THEN 'Post‑Retirement'
        ELSE                                               'On‑Track'
    END AS overtake_type,
    COUNT(*) AS num_events
FROM (
    /* plug in de‑pit‑filtered candidate overtakes from query 9 */
    /* … the same CTE content as in query 9 … */
) AS co
LEFT JOIN pit_stops AS ps_prev
       ON ps_prev.race_id = co.race_id
      AND ps_prev.driver_id = co.driver_b
      AND ps_prev.lap = co.lap - 1
LEFT JOIN pit_stops AS ps_curr
       ON ps_curr.race_id = co.race_id
      AND ps_curr.driver_id = co.driver_a
      AND ps_curr.lap = co.lap
LEFT JOIN retirements AS ret_prev
       ON ret_prev.race_id = co.race_id
      AND ret_prev.driver_id = co.driver_b
      AND ret_prev.lap = co.lap - 1
GROUP BY overtake_type
ORDER BY num_events DESC;
```

Each query is capped with `LIMIT 20` (where applicable) to keep inspection results manageable.  
Running them in sequence lets you go from basic data discovery to a draft aggregation that counts how many overtakes fall into each logical bucket (race start, pure on‑track, pit‑in, pit‑out, or retirement‑driven).
